413Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-22T15:24:42.413Z"}],"createdAt":"2026-07-24T17:30:06.027Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-22T15:24:38.980Z"},{"id":"cnt_c61fc50ef50c200aafd02da70ebe","matchedQueries":[],"platform":"instagram","profileHandle":"ladbible","profileDisplayName":"LADbible","format":"video","caption":"Need to know how this started 😂⁠\n⁠\nAnimals sploot, lying flat with their back legs stretched out behind them for a few practical reasons, and it’s completely normal for many species. One of the main reasons is temperature regulation. By pressing their belly against a cool surface like tiles, grass, or concrete, animals can lose body heat more effectively. Splooting can also be a comfortable stretching position, helping relax the hips and leg muscles after running or playing. It’s especially common in flexible animals such as dogs, cats, squirrels, and frogs.⁠\n⁠\nSome breeds and species are more likely to sploot because of their body shape and joint flexibility. Young animals often do it more frequently, as they tend to have greater flexibility than older ones. As long as the animal appears comfortable and can move normally afterwards, splooting is simply a relaxed resting position, and one of the internet’s favourite animal behaviours.⁠\n⁠\n🎥: CONTENTbible⁠\n⁠\n#cats #animals #ladbible","captionLanguage":"en","thumbnailMediaId":"med_69bf0ce526848a03cf35a1e91cb2","publishedAt":"2026-07-08T10:03:30.000Z","profileId":"prf_5a8c502a8b656a1f244434854e0b","thumbnailMediaUrl":"https://oriane-contents.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/instagram/DahxvT1kbe9/thumbnail.webp","viewsCount":7629810,"likesCount":547071,"sharesCount":204166,"commentsCount":1144,"interactionsCount":752381,"engagementRatePerViews":9.86107124554871,"engagementRatePerFollowers":4.940770006010634,"profileFollowersCount":15228011,"profileFollowingCount":81,"profilePostsCount":44577,"mediaCount":1,"duration":13.333,"hashtags":["#ladbible","#animals","#cats"],"coAuthors":[],"mentions":[],"platformId":"DahxvT1kbe9","prof